    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d221">

                <pb facs="0232" n="220" sameAs="frus:frus1943v05/pg220"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">814.24/401: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Guatemala</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Drew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Guatemala</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1943-02-04T17:00:00-06:00">February 4, 1943—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 9:40 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>57. For Parkhurst Board of Economic Warfare<anchor n="24" xml:id="d221-f1"/> and
                    Ravndal of the Department<anchor n="25" xml:id="d221-f2"/> from Dall [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Dalley</hi>] and Moseley.<anchor n="26" xml:id="d221-f3"
                    /> Mission and Country Agency<anchor n="27" xml:id="d221-f4"/> will be ready to
                    put Decentralization Plan “A”<anchor n="28" xml:id="d221-f5"/> into effect for
                    all materials on April 1. Beginning March 1 preference requests will be issued
                    in lieu of Certificates of Necessity on allocated materials for second quarter.
                    It is suggested that outstanding Certificates of Necessity be honored until
                    April 30. Thenceforth all applications for export license should be accompanied
                    by preference requests.</p>
                <p>Send timing and recapitulation of all materials under quota and CMP<anchor n="29"
                        xml:id="d221-f6"/> with full instructions and estimate of supply.</p>
                <p>No great difficulty is anticipated in Guatemala in the operation of this plan.
                    [Dalley and Moseley.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Drew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d221-f1" n="24" xml:id="d221-f1-content">
                <p>George V. Parkhurst, Deputy Chief, Country Program Branch.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d221-f2" n="25" xml:id="d221-f2-content">
                <p>Christian M. Ravndal, Chief of the Division of Exports and Requirements.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d221-f3" n="26" xml:id="d221-f3-content">
                <p>J. A. R. Dalley and Harold W. Moseley, the former representing the Board of
                    Economic Warfare and the latter, the American Hemisphere Exports Office, were
                    assigned to assist in inaugurating a decentralized export control plan.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d221-f4" n="27" xml:id="d221-f4-content">
                <p>The Guatemalan office responsible for administering import controls.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d221-f5" n="28" xml:id="d221-f5-content">
                <p>For an outline of the plan, see circular airgram, January 19, 8 p.m., <ref
                        target="#pg106">p. 106</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d221-f6" n="29" xml:id="d221-f6-content">
                <p>Controlled Materials Plan; for an explanation of this plan, see circular
                    instruction of April 26, <ref target="#pg111">p. 111</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
